{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אשת משה אחר שלוחיה", :translit "eshet moshe acher sheluchiah", :gloss "the wife of Moses after he had sent her away"} {:he "היא כאשתו", :translit "hi ke-ishto", :gloss "she is like his wife"} {:he "אע\"פ ששלחה", :translit "af al pi she-shelachah", :gloss "even though he sent her"} {:he "שמלך אין נושאין גרושתו", :translit "she-melech ein nos'in gerushato", :gloss "for a king may not marry his divorcee"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Halakhic inference: a king who divorces his wife may never remarry her, so Moses's estranged wife remains fully his wife."}], :frame {:text "The Torah restricts a king from marrying a divorcee: 'he shall not multiply wives' (Deut 17:17) is interpreted by the Sages to prohibit a king from marrying a woman previously divorced, even if she had been divorced from him personally (Sanhedrin 18a, 21a). Moses held the status of a king (Zevachim 102a, comparing Moses to a king via the verse 'You shall set a king over you,' Deut 17:15), so the standard rules of divorce and remarriage did not apply to him.", :sources ["Deut 17:17" "Deut 17:15" "Sanhedrin 18a" "Sanhedrin 21a" "Zevachim 102a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Halakhic inference: a king who divorces his wife may never remarry her, so Moses's estranged wife remains fully his wife.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:17:05.978640210Z", :model "glm-5.2"}